From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: John Ericson <John.Ericson@Obsidian.Systems>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 01:25:17 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] tools/libcompat: build and install it, instead of every
 consumer rebuilding it

`tools/libcompat` was created as a directory of headers and one C file with no
Makefile, referenced only through `$(COMPAT_CPPFLAGS)` and
`$(COMPAT_SUPPORT_SRC)` in `Makefile.tools`.  That leaves each consumer to
compile `native_support.c` for itself, and leaves the "ELF-y" header staging --
the thing that makes gate source compile against a foreign libc at all -- with
nowhere to live, so every consumer reimplements it.  Give it a Makefile and put
it in `SUBDIRS`, ahead of everything else in `BOOT_SUBDIRS`, so `ctf` and `sgs`
can both rely on it.

Each half is shaped after an existing precedent in this tree rather than
invented:

  * The header half follows `tools/sgs/include/Makefile` -- a headers-only
    directory in `tools/` whose `all install:` gathers a hand-picked list out of
    `$(SRC)/uts` with `$(INS.file)`, and which is itself in `SUBDIRS`.  As
    there, the point is to keep the whole illumos header tree off the include
    path, where `<sys/types.h>` and friends collide head-on with the host
    libc's.

  * The library half follows `tools/ctf/libctf/Makefile.com` -- build it once
    and install under `$(ROOTONBLDLIBMACH)`, which `Makefile.tools` already
    defines alongside `$(ROOTONBLDLIB)`.

The gathered headers go in `include/`, NOT in `common/`, and that separation is
load-bearing.  `common/sys/elf.h` is a shim forwarding to the *host's*
`<elf.h>`, for the CTF tools, which link the host libelf; the gathered
`include/sys/elf.h` is illumos' real one, for tools that want illumos' ELF
types.  A consumer chooses by which directory it `-I`s first, and merging the
two would make that choice unexpressible.  It is also why `.gitignore` gains
`libcompat/include` and not `libcompat/common`: `common/` is source, `include/`
is generated -- compare the existing `sgs/include/sys` entry.

The header list carries a warning that is the real motivation here.  These
headers depend on each other in ways the list does not show: staging
`<sys/auxv.h>` without also staging `<sys/auxv_386.h>` breaks `elfextract`, and
nothing about the list says so.  `tools/sgs/include/Makefile` names both
together for exactly that reason.  A hand-maintained copy of this list
elsewhere gets it wrong sooner or later -- one already did, and was backed out
for it.  Having one list, in the tree that owns the headers, removes that class
of bug by construction rather than by care.

No consumer is switched over here.  `$(COMPAT_CPPFLAGS)` and
`$(COMPAT_SUPPORT_SRC)` still work exactly as before, so `ctf` and `sgs` build
unchanged; moving them to link `-lcompat` is a separate change.

Assisted-By: Claude Opus 5 (claude-opus-5)
---
 usr/src/tools/.gitignore         |   1 +
 usr/src/tools/Makefile           |   2 +
 usr/src/tools/libcompat/Makefile | 148 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 3 files changed, 151 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 usr/src/tools/libcompat/Makefile

diff --git a/usr/src/tools/.gitignore b/usr/src/tools/.gitignore
index 2d5bffaeb4..2334bb48dd 100644
--- a/usr/src/tools/.gitignore
+++ b/usr/src/tools/.gitignore
@@ -20,6 +20,7 @@ install-*.out
 __pycache__
 
 sgs/include/sys
+libcompat/include
 sgs/*/*.[chs]
 !sgs/sgsmsg/sgsmsg.c
 !libcompat/common/*.[chs]
diff --git a/usr/src/tools/Makefile b/usr/src/tools/Makefile
index 0007c61eb3..8f06bc4e04 100644
--- a/usr/src/tools/Makefile
+++ b/usr/src/tools/Makefile
@@ -42,6 +42,8 @@ include ../Makefile.master
 # installation rules in their makefiles. This includes the Makefile for
 # 'install.bin' itself.
 BOOT_SUBDIRS=		\
+	libcompat	\
+	.WAIT		\
 	smatch		\
 	.WAIT		\
 	cw		\
diff --git a/usr/src/tools/libcompat/Makefile b/usr/src/tools/libcompat/Makefile
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000..d228f92b2e
--- /dev/null
+++ b/usr/src/tools/libcompat/Makefile
@@ -0,0 +1,148 @@
+#
+# This file and its contents are supplied under the terms of the
+# Common Development and Distribution License ("CDDL"), version 1.0.
+# You may only use this file in accordance with the terms of version
+# 1.0 of the CDDL.
+#
+# A full copy of the text of the CDDL should have accompanied this
+# source.  A copy of the CDDL is also available via the Internet at
+# http://www.illumos.org/license/CDDL.
+#
+
+#
+# Copyright 2026 Oxide Computer Company
+#
+
+include ../Makefile.tools
+
+#
+# libcompat has two halves, and they are shaped after two different existing
+# things in this tree, because each half already has a precedent here:
+#
+#  * The header half follows tools/sgs/include/Makefile: a headers-only
+#    directory in tools/ whose `all install' target gathers a hand-picked list
+#    of "ELF-y" headers out of the workspace with $(INS.file), into a directory
+#    the build then searches with -I.  As there, the point is to avoid putting
+#    the whole illumos header tree on the include path, where <sys/types.h> and
+#    friends collide head-on with the host libc's.
+#
+#  * The library half follows tools/ctf/libctf/Makefile.com: build an object
+#    and install it under $(ROOTONBLDLIBMACH), so that consumers link it rather
+#    than each compiling the source again.  ($(ROOTONBLDLIB) and
+#    $(ROOTONBLDLIBMACH) are defined by Makefile.tools.)
+#
+# The two output directories are deliberately NOT the same, and this is the
+# subtle part.  common/ holds the checked-in shims, and include/ holds the
+# headers gathered out of the workspace.  They must stay apart because
+# common/sys/elf.h is a shim that forwards to the *host's* <elf.h> -- for the
+# CTF tools, which link the host libelf -- while the gathered include/sys/elf.h
+# is illumos' real one, for the tools that want illumos' ELF types.  A consumer
+# picks a side by which directory it -I's first.  Merging them would make that
+# choice unexpressible.
+#
+# That split is also why the .gitignore entry is `libcompat/include' and not
+# `libcompat/common': common/ is source, include/ is generated.  Compare
+# `sgs/include/sys', ignored for exactly this reason.
+#
+
+#
+# The gathered header list.  Judgement is required to extend it: prefer letting
+# the host libc win, and add a header only when the host genuinely has no
+# counterpart, or -- as with <sys/elf.h> -- when the host's counterpart refuses
+# to compile at all.
+#
+# A WARNING worth heeding, because it has already cost one backed-out change:
+# these headers have dependencies on each other that are not visible in the
+# list.  Staging <sys/auxv.h> without also staging <sys/auxv_386.h> breaks
+# elfextract, and nothing about the list says so.  tools/sgs/include/Makefile
+# names both together for precisely this reason.  Neither is needed here today;
+# if you add one, add the other.
+#
+SYSHDRS=	sys/elf.h \
+		sys/elftypes.h \
+		sys/elf_386.h \
+		sys/elf_amd64.h \
+		sys/elf_SPARC.h \
+		sys/elf_notes.h \
+		sys/isa_defs.h \
+		sys/feature_tests.h \
+		sys/ccompile.h \
+		sys/note.h \
+		sys/multiboot.h \
+		sys/multiboot2.h \
+		sys/queue.h \
+		sys/types32.h \
+		sys/containerof.h \
+		sys/stddef.h
+
+#
+# <sys/machtypes.h> is per-ISA; everything above is machine-independent.  The
+# ISA directory under uts/ is not spelled $(MACH) -- x86 is `intel' there,
+# while $(MACH) is `i386' -- so map it.
+#
+i386_UTSDIR=	intel
+sparc_UTSDIR=	sparc
+UTSDIR=		$($(MACH)_UTSDIR)
+
+MACHSYSHDRS=	sys/machtypes.h
+
+HDRS=		$(SYSHDRS:%=include/%) $(MACHSYSHDRS:%=include/%)
+
+LIBRARY=	libcompat.a
+OBJS=		native_support.o
+
+CPPFLAGS +=	$(COMPAT_CPPFLAGS)
+
+.KEEP_STATE:
+
+all: $(HDRS) $(LIBRARY)
+
+include/sys:
+	$(MKDIR) -p include/sys
+
+include/sys/%: include/sys $(SRC)/uts/common/sys/%
+	$(RM) $@; $(CP) $(SRC)/uts/common/sys/$(@F) $@; $(CHMOD) 644 $@
+
+include/sys/machtypes.h: include/sys $(SRC)/uts/$(UTSDIR)/sys/machtypes.h
+	$(RM) $@; $(CP) $(SRC)/uts/$(UTSDIR)/sys/machtypes.h $@; $(CHMOD) 644 $@
+
+#
+# <sys/types32.h> is nothing but the int32_t/uint32_t typedefs, but it reaches
+# them through illumos' <sys/int_types.h>, which redefines the whole intN_t
+# family and so collides with the host's <stdint.h>.  Point it at <stdint.h>;
+# the typedefs it defines are unchanged either way.
+#
+include/sys/types32.h: include/sys $(SRC)/uts/common/sys/types32.h
+	$(RM) $@
+	$(SED) -e 's|<sys/int_types\.h>|<stdint.h>|' \
+	    $(SRC)/uts/common/sys/types32.h > $@
+	$(CHMOD) 644 $@
+
+native_support.o: common/native_support.c
+	$(NATIVECC) $(NATIVE_CFLAGS) $(CPPFLAGS) -c -o $@ common/native_support.c
+
+#
+# $(AR) is deliberately the *build host's* ar, not the target's: libcompat is
+# linked into tools that run on the build machine.  Makefile.master defines
+# ARFLAGS; AR itself falls back to make's builtin `ar'.
+#
+$(LIBRARY): $(OBJS)
+	$(RM) $@; $(AR) $(ARFLAGS) $@ $(OBJS)
+
+install: all $(ROOTONBLDLIBMACH)/$(LIBRARY)
+
+$(ROOTONBLDLIBMACH):
+	$(MKDIR) -p $@
+
+$(ROOTONBLDLIBMACH)/$(LIBRARY): $(ROOTONBLDLIBMACH) $(LIBRARY)
+	$(RM) $@; $(CP) $(LIBRARY) $@; $(CHMOD) 644 $@
+
+clean:
+	$(RM) $(OBJS) $(LIBRARY)
+
+clobber: clean
+	$(RM) -r include
+
+FRC:
+
+include ../Makefile.targ
